WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.880
Freezing and the pressure rise arrive together.

2
00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:09.400
Do they travel one route out of the amygdala, or two?

3
00:00:10.383 --> 00:00:15.283
In 1988, Joseph LeDoux and his colleagues asked exactly that, in rats.

4
00:00:16.367 --> 00:00:19.567
First they damaged one target of the central amygdala.

5
00:00:20.567 --> 00:00:24.127
Two weeks later they trained the rats, and the next day played the tone alone.

6
00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:30.020
In the same rats, they measured both responses: freezing, and the rise in blood

7
00:00:30.020 --> 00:00:31.020
pressure.

8
00:00:31.433 --> 00:00:33.313
Picture two bars for each group.

9
00:00:34.233 --> 00:00:36.653
Intact rats that had the pairing show two tall bars.

10
00:00:37.617 --> 00:00:40.457
Intact rats without the pairing show two short bars.

11
00:00:41.217 --> 00:00:42.597
That short pair is our baseline.

12
00:00:43.717 --> 00:00:48.857
For these invented numbers, we assume the damage leaves the baseline alone, and that

13
00:00:48.857 --> 00:00:51.197
each score is the baseline plus a learned part.

14
00:00:52.057 --> 00:00:55.237
So the excess over the baseline estimates the learned response.

15
00:00:56.300 --> 00:01:00.840
Suppose both responses need the same route through these lateral hypothalamus

16
00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:03.600
neurons. What should damage there do to freezing?

17
00:01:12.733 --> 00:01:16.033
If both responses needed those neurons, both would fall.

18
00:01:16.973 --> 00:01:20.633
They do not. The pressure rise falls almost to the baseline.

19
00:01:21.767 --> 00:01:23.827
Freezing stays almost at the top.

20
00:01:24.687 --> 00:01:28.987
So freezing does not need the lateral hypothalamus, but the pressure rise does.

21
00:01:30.050 --> 00:01:33.870
Now damage the caudal, or rear, periaqueductal gray instead.

22
00:01:34.710 --> 00:01:35.890
What do you expect this time?

23
00:01:44.883 --> 00:01:50.243
The reverse. Freezing drops almost to the baseline, and the pressure rise survives.

24
00:01:51.333 --> 00:01:54.973
Each kind of damage impairs one response and spares the other.

25
00:01:55.853 --> 00:01:57.993
That pattern is called a double dissociation.

26
00:01:59.117 --> 00:02:03.617
It means different targets below one amygdala node contribute to different

27
00:02:03.617 --> 00:02:04.617
responses.

28
00:02:05.267 --> 00:02:10.327
In the worked problem, damage to the central amygdala itself leaves neither learned

29
00:02:10.327 --> 00:02:13.587
response above the baseline, as a shared source predicts.

30
00:02:14.667 --> 00:02:16.047
One detail matters.

31
00:02:16.887 --> 00:02:22.227
The caudal midbrain damage used ibotenic acid, a toxin that kills cell bodies but

32
00:02:22.227 --> 00:02:23.607
spares passing axons.

33
00:02:24.650 --> 00:02:29.430
So the freezing loss points to neurons in the periaqueductal gray, not to cut

34
00:02:29.430 --> 00:02:35.150
fibres. The same toxin in the lateral hypothalamus impaired the pressure rise while

35
00:02:35.150 --> 00:02:36.150
sparing freezing.

36
00:02:37.083 --> 00:02:40.003
This experiment finds the routes out of the amygdala.

37
00:02:40.963 --> 00:02:42.603
It says nothing yet about a feeling.
